    question: "What is SFT (Supervised Fine-Tuning)? What data and loss does it use?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Fine-tune pretrained LLM on demonstration data (human-written prompt→response pairs)
• Loss: cross-entropy on response tokens only (mask out prompt tokens in some implementations)
• Data sourcing: human contractors writing ideal answers, or distillation from stronger models
• Purpose: teach instruction-following format; narrows behavior from "predict internet text" to "be a helpful assistant"`
  },
  {
    id: "train-3",
    theme: "LLM Training Pipeline",
    question: "Explain RLHF end-to-end. What is the reward model and how is PPO applied?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
1. Collect preference data: sample two model outputs for same prompt, human labels which is better
2. Train reward model r_φ(x,y) with Bradley-Terry loss: log σ(r(x,y_w) − r(x,y_l))
3. Fine-tune LLM with PPO:
   • Sample prompt x, generate y ~ π_θ
   • Score with r_φ; compute advantages
   • Update θ to maximize E[r(x,y)] − β · KL(π_θ(y|x) || π_ref(y|x))
   • KL penalty (β) prevents reward hacking / model collapse`
  },
  {
    id: "train-4",
    theme: "LLM Training Pipeline",
    question: "What is DPO and how does it differ from RLHF with PPO?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Direct Preference Optimization (Rafailov et al. 2023) — no explicit reward model, no RL loop
• Derives closed-form loss from RLHF objective under the optimal policy:
  L = -E[ log σ( β log(π_θ(y_w|x)/π_ref(y_w|x)) − β log(π_θ(y_l|x)/π_ref(y_l|x)) ) ]
• Train directly on (prompt, chosen, rejected) triples
• Advantages: simpler, more stable, same GPU as SFT; no reward hacking
• Tradeoffs: slightly less flexible, no online data collection`
