  {
    id: "attn-1",
    theme: "Attention & Efficiency",
    question: "What is the computational and memory complexity of self-attention? Why is it a bottleneck?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Time: O(n²·d) — QKᵀ is n×n matrix of dot products, each O(d)
• Memory: O(n²) — must store full attention matrix for backward pass
• Bottleneck: quadratic in sequence length; 4K tokens → 16M scores; 100K tokens → 10B scores
• Motivates FlashAttention, linear attention, sparse attention, state-space models (Mamba)`
  },
  {
    id: "attn-2",
    theme: "Attention & Efficiency",
    question: "What is FlashAttention and how does it achieve memory efficiency?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• IO-aware exact attention algorithm (Dao et al. 2022)
• Never materializes the full n×n attention matrix in HBM (GPU main memory)
• Tiles Q, K, V into SRAM blocks; fuses softmax + matmul in a single kernel
• Uses online softmax trick to compute correct result incrementally
• Memory: O(n) instead of O(n²); same arithmetic as standard attention
• Speedup: 2–4× on A100 due to reduced memory bandwidth`
  },
  {
    id: "attn-3",
    theme: "Attention & Efficiency",
    question: "What is the KV cache? Why is it critical for autoregressive inference?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• During generation, K and V for all past tokens are fixed — no need to recompute
• Cache K, V tensors per layer; on each new token only compute Q for new position, attend over cached K/V
• Reduces per-step cost from O(n²) to O(n·d) — each step is linear in context length
• Memory cost: 2 × n × d_model × n_layers × sizeof(dtype) per sequence
• E.g., Llama-3 70B with 32K context ≈ ~32GB KV cache in fp16 — often the inference bottleneck`
  },
