    question: "What is perplexity? What does it measure and what are its limitations?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• PPL = exp( −(1/n) Σ log p(wᵢ | w₁…wᵢ₋₁) ) = exp(cross-entropy loss)
• Geometric mean inverse probability assigned to each token — lower is better
• Measures how well the model predicts the test distribution
• Limitations:
  - Sensitive to tokenization (can't compare across different tokenizers)
  - Doesn't measure instruction-following, factuality, or helpfulness
  - Low PPL ≠ useful model (a fine-tuned model may have higher PPL but be more helpful)`
