  {
    id: "infer-1",
    theme: "Inference & Decoding",
    question: "Compare greedy decoding, beam search, top-k sampling, and nucleus (top-p) sampling.",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Greedy: argmax at each step; fast, deterministic; often repetitive/degenerate
• Beam search: keep top-b sequences at each step; better quality for constrained tasks (translation); not great for open-ended generation (generic outputs)
• Top-k: sample from top k tokens by probability; fixed k can be too broad (flat dist) or too narrow (peaked dist)
• Nucleus (top-p): sample from smallest set of tokens whose cumulative probability ≥ p; adapts to distribution sharpness; typical p=0.9–0.95
• Temperature: divide logits by T before softmax; T>1 flattens (more random), T<1 sharpens (more deterministic)`
  },
  {
    id: "infer-2",
    theme: "Inference & Decoding",
    question: "What is speculative decoding and how does it speed up inference without quality loss?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Problem: large model inference is memory-bandwidth bound at batch size 1 — small draft model is much faster
• Algorithm:
  1. Draft model generates k candidate tokens autoregressively (cheap)
  2. Large model scores all k+1 positions in ONE forward pass (parallelized)
  3. Accept tokens greedily up to first mismatch using rejection sampling; resample remainder
• Result: same output distribution as large model alone; typical speedup 2–3×
• Requires draft and target to share tokenizer; draft must be similar distribution`
  },

  // ── ML Fundamentals ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {
    id: "ml-1",
    theme: "ML Fundamentals",
    question: "Explain the bias-variance tradeoff. How does it manifest in neural networks?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Bias: error from wrong assumptions in the model (underfitting)
• Variance: error from sensitivity to training data fluctuations (overfitting)
• Total error ≈ Bias² + Variance + Irreducible noise
• In neural nets: larger/deeper models → lower bias, higher variance → need more data / regularization
• Modern observation: overparameterized NNs can interpolate training data yet generalize (double descent)
• Regularization tools: dropout, weight decay, early stopping, data augmentation`
  },
  {
    id: "ml-2",
    theme: "ML Fundamentals",
    question: "What is batch normalization? What problem does it solve and what are its drawbacks?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• Normalizes layer activations to zero mean, unit variance per mini-batch; then learnable scale γ and shift β
• Solves: internal covariate shift, allows higher learning rates, acts as regularizer
• Drawbacks: depends on batch size (bad for small batches or online settings); different behavior train vs inference (running stats); doesn't work well for sequence models
• Alternatives: LayerNorm (used in Transformers) — normalizes across feature dim per sample; independent of batch size`
  },
  {
    id: "ml-3",
    theme: "ML Fundamentals",
    question: "What is the vanishing gradient problem and how do residual connections solve it?",
    answer:
`TODO — key points:
• In deep networks, gradients are products of Jacobians through many layers; if values < 1, product → 0 exponentially
• Residual connection: y = F(x) + x → gradient through shortcut = 1 (additive, not multiplicative)
• Gradient of loss w.r.t. x = ∂L/∂y · (∂F/∂x + I) — always has I term so gradient can flow
• Enables training of very deep networks (ResNet, Transformers)
• Related: LSTM gates address vanishing gradient for RNNs`
  },

